sections.body:
A good habit: keep **calculating** and **drawing** in separate functions.

```js
const ball = { x: 20, y: 60, r: 10, vx: 2 };

function update() {
  ball.x += ball.vx;  // only changes numbers
}

function draw() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(ball.x, ball.y, ball.r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fill();         // only draws
}
```

That way you can call `update()` on its own and check the result, and when the game is paused you can call only `draw()`. Calling functions one at a time is also the easiest way to test them.

**Speed** (`vx`, `vy`) is how much the position changes every frame. If `vx = 3`, the object moves 3 pixels right every frame; with `vx = -3`, it moves left.

To make it come back when it hits an edge, we flip the sign of the speed:

```js
if (ball.x + ball.r > canvas.width || ball.x - ball.r < 0) {
  ball.vx = -ball.vx;
}
```

If the object went a little past the edge, it's also good to put it back at the edge (**clamping**). `Math.max` and `Math.min` are the short way: `x = Math.max(0, Math.min(x, canvas.width - w));`

Some screens draw 60 frames per second, some 120. If you write the speed "per frame", the game plays twice as fast on the faster screen! The fix: write the speed **per second** and multiply it by the time between two frames (`dt`). rAF gives the function the time in milliseconds:

```js
let last = 0;
function loop(time) {
  if (!last) last = time;            // the first frame
  const dt = (time - last) / 1000;   // seconds
  last = time;
  rocket.x += rocket.speed * dt;     // speed: pixels per second
  draw();
  frameId = requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
```

`requestAnimationFrame` returns a number. `cancelAnimationFrame(frameId)` cancels the frame with that number, and the loop stops.

examples.code:
const canvas = document.querySelector("#c");
const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
const box = { x: 0, y: 50, size: 20, vx: 2 };

function update() {
  box.x += box.vx;
  if (box.x > canvas.width) box.x = -box.size; // leave on the right, come in on the left
}

function draw() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  ctx.fillStyle = "#4fc3f7";
  ctx.fillRect(box.x, box.y, box.size, box.size);
}

function loop() {
  update();
  draw();
  requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
requestAnimationFrame(loop);

const canvas = document.querySelector("#c");
const ctx = canvas.getContext("2d");
const ball = { x: 50, y: 40, r: 10, vx: 2.5, vy: 1.5 };

function update() {
  ball.x += ball.vx;
  ball.y += ball.vy;
  if (ball.x + ball.r > canvas.width || ball.x - ball.r < 0) ball.vx = -ball.vx;
  if (ball.y + ball.r > canvas.height || ball.y - ball.r < 0) ball.vy = -ball.vy;
}

function draw() {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.arc(ball.x, ball.y, ball.r, 0, Math.PI * 2);
  ctx.fillStyle = "#ff9800";
  ctx.fill();
}

function loop() {
  update();
  draw();
  requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
requestAnimationFrame(loop);

